/* Project and behavioral questions. Frameworks + sample answers built only from facts on the resume.
   Anything in [brackets] must be filled in with your own true details. Never invent numbers or experience. */
(function () {
  const B = (q, k, a, t) => ({ lv: 'Behavioral', q, k, a, t });
  HUB.add({
    id: 'behavioral', title: 'Project and behavioral questions', short: 'Behavioral',
    blurb: 'Answer frameworks for "tell me about…" questions, with sample answers built from your resume. Replace every [bracket] with your own true details, then practise saying them out loud.',
    items: [
B(`Tell me about yourself.`,
  `A short story: who you are now, what you've built, and what you want next.`,
  `<b>Structure (60–90 seconds): present → past → why this role.</b><ol><li><b>Present:</b> "I'm a React.js developer with 6+ years in IT. For the last [3+] years at MITI Sphere Solutions I've worked on Signulu, a cloud e-signature platform, building reusable React components from InVision designs, integrating APIs with the backend team and shipping through Azure DevOps."</li><li><b>Past:</b> "Before that I built a shipyard progress-tracking app (Shipcom) with React, Redux and Ant Design at Do Points Marketing, and started at Predifast on a React + Redux job portal and an ASP.NET MVC education system, where I also did requirement analysis."</li><li><b>Next:</b> "I'm looking for [a role where I can own frontend features end to end / work on larger-scale React architecture], which is why this role interests me."</li></ol>`,
  `Don't recite the whole resume. Pick one achievement you can explain in depth if they ask. Practise until it sounds natural, not memorised.`),
B(`Explain your current project and its architecture.`,
  `Describe what the app does, how the screen talks to the server, and your part in it.`,
  `<b>Structure:</b> product → users → frontend architecture → your responsibilities → one challenge.<ol><li><b>Product:</b> "Signulu lets individuals and businesses sign documents online from any device, with encryption and legally binding signatures."</li><li><b>Architecture:</b> "The frontend is React with ES6 [and state management: Redux / Context / which one]. We build screens from InVision designs as reusable components, call REST APIs provided by the backend team, and deploy through Azure DevOps CI/CD; tasks are tracked as user stories in Azure Boards."</li><li><b>Your part:</b> "I [build feature screens such as …], create reusable components, coordinate API contracts with backend and fix issues reported by QA."</li><li><b>Challenge:</b> "[One real problem: e.g. a complex document-signing flow, a performance or browser issue] and how you solved it."</li></ol>`,
  `Have a simple diagram in your head: UI components → API layer → backend services. Be ready for follow-ups on folder structure, state management choices and how you handle errors and loading.`),
B(`What is your hands-on experience with TypeScript in real projects?`,
  `Tell the truth about how much you've used it, and show how you'd use it well.`,
  `<b>Be honest; interviewers compare answers with your resume (which currently lists JavaScript, not TypeScript).</b><ol><li><b>If you have used TypeScript at work:</b> name the project, what you typed (API response types, component props, Redux slices), one specific benefit ("it caught a renamed API field at compile time") and one challenge (typing third-party libraries or generic components). Then add it to your resume.</li><li><b>If your projects were in JavaScript:</b> say so plainly, then show real readiness: "Our projects were JavaScript, so my production TypeScript is limited. I've been learning it seriously: [course / side project / this prep]. I understand typing props and hooks, unions and narrowing, generics, and utility types like Omit and Partial. In a team I'd start by typing API responses and component props, use strict mode, and avoid <code>any</code>."</li><li><b>Show the "why":</b> safer refactors, self-documenting components, earlier bug detection, better editor support.</li></ol>`,
  `A good honest answer beats a weak invented one. Interviewers often probe with "How did you type X?" Back up every claim with one concrete example you can explain.`),
B(`What is your approach to writing test cases?`,
  `Test what the user does and sees, the most important flows first.`,
  `<b>Explain your strategy, then your real experience.</b><ol><li><b>What to test:</b> critical user flows first (login, signing a document, payments), then complex logic and bug-prone areas; test behaviour, not implementation details.</li><li><b>Levels:</b> unit tests for pure functions and reducers; component/integration tests with Jest or Vitest + React Testing Library (query by role, user-event); MSW to mock APIs, including error and empty states; a few end-to-end tests with Playwright or Cypress for critical journeys.</li><li><b>Each test:</b> arrange → act → assert, one behaviour per test, readable names ("shows an error when the upload fails").</li><li><b>Process:</b> write a failing test for every bug fix, run tests in CI before merge, and work with QA so automated tests cover what they check manually.</li><li><b>Your experience:</b> "[Describe what testing you actually did: unit tests, manual testing with the QA team, reviewing test cases]. Where our project relied on manual QA, I [worked closely with QA / wrote test scenarios] and I'm now [practising RTL and Playwright]."</li></ol>`,
  `If you haven't written many automated tests, say so and show a clear plan; then be ready to write a small RTL test live.`),
B(`How do you handle PR reviews and feedback from senior developers?`,
  `Treat feedback as help to make the code better, ask questions, and learn from it.`,
  `<b>Structure: mindset → as an author → as a reviewer → example.</b><ol><li><b>Mindset:</b> "Reviews are about the code and the product, not about me. Senior feedback is the fastest way to grow."</li><li><b>As the author:</b> keep PRs small and focused, write a clear description (what, why, how to test, screenshots), self-review first, respond to every comment, ask "why" when unsure instead of silently changing, and discuss on a call when a thread gets long.</li><li><b>When you disagree:</b> explain your reasoning with facts (performance, readability, requirements), accept the team's decision, and follow conventions.</li><li><b>As a reviewer:</b> check correctness, edge cases, readability, performance, accessibility and tests; be specific and kind; separate must-fix from suggestions.</li><li><b>Example:</b> "[A real time a senior pointed out something, e.g. a reusable component or an effect problem; what you changed and what you learned]."</li></ol>`,
  `Interviewers look for humility plus confidence: you accept feedback, you can also defend a choice politely, and you learn from patterns in feedback.`),
B(`What is your process for gathering and understanding client requirements?`,
  `Ask lots of questions, write down what you understood, and check it before building.`,
  `<b>Use your real experience:</b> your resume includes requirement gathering and analysis, impact analysis and effort documents, updating functional requirement documents, daily stand-ups and building UIs from InVision designs.<ol><li><b>Understand the goal:</b> who the users are, what problem they have, what "done" looks like.</li><li><b>Clarify details:</b> walk through the InVision designs and user stories; ask about edge cases (empty, error, loading states), validations, permissions, devices and browsers, and performance expectations.</li><li><b>Confirm with the backend:</b> agree on API contracts (fields, errors, pagination) early.</li><li><b>Document:</b> write acceptance criteria or update the functional document; do an impact analysis and effort estimate for change requests.</li><li><b>Validate early:</b> demo work in progress, get feedback, and flag risks or scope changes early instead of at the deadline.</li></ol><b>Example:</b> "At Predifast I prepared impact analysis and effort documents for change requests and updated the functional requirement documents; at MITI Sphere I gather requirements in stand-ups and from InVision designs, then [example of a question you asked that avoided rework]."`,
  `Show that you reduce ambiguity before coding. A short example of a clarifying question that saved rework is very convincing.`),
B(`Describe a difficult production bug and how you investigated it.`,
  `Tell the story of a tricky problem: what broke, how you found the cause, and how you stopped it happening again.`,
  `<b>STAR framework:</b> <b>Situation</b> (what users saw, impact) → <b>Task</b> (your role) → <b>Action</b> (how you reproduced it, tools used: DevTools Network/Console, React DevTools, logs; how you narrowed the cause; the fix) → <b>Result</b> (what improved, how you prevented recurrence: test, monitoring, code review rule). Fill with a real bug: "[e.g. an API called twice, data not refreshing after save, a layout breaking on one browser, a signing step failing for large documents]".`,
  `Spend most of the time on the investigation steps, which shows your debugging skill. Use real numbers only if you actually measured them.`),
B(`Tell me about a performance issue you solved.`,
  `Something was slow; how you measured it, what you changed, and how you checked it got better.`,
  `<b>Measure → find cause → fix → verify.</b> "[Screen/feature] was slow when [condition]. I measured with [React Profiler / Performance tab / Lighthouse], found [cause: too many re-renders, large list, big bundle, repeated API calls], fixed it with [memoization, virtualization or pagination, code splitting, caching/debouncing], and confirmed by [re-measuring / user feedback]." If you haven't solved a major one, describe a smaller real improvement and explain how you'd approach a larger one.`,
  `Never invent percentages. "It went from noticeably laggy to smooth, confirmed in the Profiler" is fine if true.`),
B(`Describe a technical decision you made and its trade-offs.`,
  `Explain a choice between options, why you picked one, and what you gave up.`,
  `<b>Context → options → criteria → decision → trade-offs → outcome.</b> Example topics from your experience: "[Redux vs local state for Shipcom tracking data]", "[building a reusable component vs a one-off]", "[Ant Design vs custom components]". Name what you gained (consistency, speed, maintainability) and what it cost (bundle size, learning curve, flexibility), and what you'd do differently now.`,
  `Seniority shows in the trade-offs, not the choice. Mention who you consulted and how you validated the decision.`),
B(`How do you handle tight deadlines and conflicting requirements?`,
  `Find out what matters most, talk early, and agree on what fits in the time.`,
  `Clarify priorities with the product owner or lead; break work into must-have and nice-to-have; raise risks early with options ("we can ship A by Friday, B next sprint"); keep quality on critical paths; document agreed changes in Azure Boards/user stories. For conflicting requirements, bring both stakeholders together with the facts and let the owner decide.`,
  `Avoid "I just work overtime" as the whole answer. Show communication and prioritisation.`),
B(`How do you collaborate with backend developers and QA engineers?`,
  `Agree on how the screen and server talk, and help testers find problems early.`,
  `<b>Backend:</b> agree on API contracts early (request/response shape, errors, pagination), use mock data or MSW to work in parallel, and report API issues with clear reproduction steps (request, response, expected). <b>QA:</b> share what changed and how to test it, include edge cases in the PR, reproduce reported bugs quickly, and confirm fixes together. Your resume: "I interact with the backend team for API information and with the test team to resolve issues", so give one real example.`,
  `Mention empathy and clear written communication; frontend developers sit between design, backend and QA.`),
B(`How do you explain technical decisions to non-technical stakeholders?`,
  `Explain using what they care about: users, time and risk, without jargon.`,
  `Start from the impact (user experience, delivery date, risk, cost), use a simple analogy, show a demo or screenshot instead of code, offer options with trade-offs, and confirm understanding. Example: "Instead of rebuilding the page, we can reuse the existing signing component, which saves [time] and keeps the experience consistent."`,
  `Interviewers want to see that you can make a decision understandable, not impressive.`),
B(`How do you ensure accessibility, security and maintainability in your code?`,
  `Make it usable by everyone, safe from attackers, and easy for the next developer.`,
  `<b>Accessibility:</b> semantic HTML, labels, keyboard support, focus management, contrast; test with keyboard and axe. <b>Security:</b> never trust client input, avoid <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> (sanitize if needed), safe token storage, HTTPS, no secrets in front-end code, keep dependencies updated. <b>Maintainability:</b> reusable components, clear folder structure, consistent naming, small PRs, linting/formatting, tests for critical flows, and documentation of non-obvious decisions.`,
  `For an e-signature product like Signulu, security and accessibility are especially relevant, so connect your answer to it.`),
    ],
  });
})();
